-- RecallFence: the provisioning-time assertion.
--
-- Template. apply.sh substitutes @@ROLE_LIST@@ with the quoted, comma-separated
-- list of roles this deployment manages, then aborts if any row comes back with
-- a verdict other than 'ok', or if fewer rows come back than roles were asked
-- for.
--
-- Why this exists, and why it is not decorative. The three privileges that
-- defeat RLS are all invisible from the application's point of view: a
-- superuser, a role carrying rolbypassrls, and a member of admin each read every
-- row and report no error while doing it. Two independent spikes found real
-- roles on a real cluster that trip this check:
--
--   Spike 3: the default SQL user a CockroachDB Cloud Basic cluster issues is a
--            superuser with rolbypassrls and membership in admin. Connect an
--            application as that user and RLS silently does nothing.
--
--   Spike 6: `ccloud cluster user create` grants admin BY DEFAULT, so tenant
--            roles provisioned through the vendor CLI come back as superusers
--            with rolbypassrls. A tenant created that way saw every other
--            tenant's rows and could SET ROLE into them.
--
-- The second is the worse one, because it is the path an infra script naturally
-- takes. Getting it wrong produces no symptom at all until a tenant reads
-- another tenant's memory, which is the exact failure this project exists to
-- prevent, introduced by the tool the project uses to set itself up.
--
-- Scoped to managed roles only, deliberately. A cluster-wide check would flag
-- the infra account, which is supposed to be an admin, and a check that cries
-- wolf gets commented out.

SELECT
  rolname,
  rolsuper,
  rolbypassrls,
  rolcanlogin,
  pg_has_role(rolname, 'admin', 'MEMBER') AS member_of_admin,
  CASE
    WHEN rolsuper                                THEN 'FAIL_SUPERUSER'
    WHEN rolbypassrls                            THEN 'FAIL_BYPASSRLS'
    WHEN pg_has_role(rolname, 'admin', 'MEMBER') THEN 'FAIL_ADMIN_MEMBER'
    WHEN NOT rolcanlogin                         THEN 'FAIL_CANNOT_LOGIN'
    ELSE 'ok'
  END AS verdict
FROM pg_roles
WHERE rolname IN (@@ROLE_LIST@@)
ORDER BY rolname;
